<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estates General (France)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1302">1302</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Estates-General of France</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estates General was the assembly of the three orders of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, clergy, nobility and commoners, first summoned by Philip IV in 1302. It met irregularly to grant taxes or advise the crown and was not called between 1614 and 1789. Its meeting in May 1789 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> proclaiming itself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estates-general-france.jpg" alt="Estates General (France)" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Isidore-Stanislaus Helman (1743-1806) and Charles Monnet (1732-1808) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatesgeneral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first national assembly was summoned in 1302 by King Philip IV, who was in conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>. During his reign it was assembled several times to grant him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, which became the most frequent reason for calling it. It always included the clergy, the nobility and the commoners, and the king summoned it to grant subsidies or to give aid and counsel, though its makeup and powers varied greatly.</p><p>In the 14th and first half of the 15th centuries only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a> was elected, and only by the privileged towns, the bonnes villes. The country districts were not represented, and the lay and ecclesiastical lords were chosen and summoned directly by the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 14th century the king could not in theory levy general taxation without the consent of the lords and the towns, so the Estates were summoned often. From 1355 to 1358, under John II, they controlled the voting of taxes and their administration. In the later 15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a>, aids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabelle">gabelle</a> became permanent, and the Estates lost the power of the purse.</p><p>At the Estates of 1484 the deputies sided with Anne de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a> over the regency and voted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a> for two years only, on the promise that they would be summoned again. The promise was not kept, and they did not meet again until 1560. The Wars of Religion and shortage of money then revived them, at Orleans in 1560, Pontoise in 1561, and Blois in 1576 and 1588. The meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1614 was the last for over a century and a half; dissensions between the three orders weakened it, and it dissolved before finishing its work.</p><p>The Estates had no share in legislation, which belonged to the king alone. Their initiative lay in presenting complaints and petitions, gathered in cahiers de doléances, which the king usually answered by an ordinance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Estates General met again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 5 May 1789, after an Assembly of Notables in 1787. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-estate">Third Estate</a>, about 25 million people, had 578 representatives, but voting by orders meant that its votes counted no more than those of each smaller estate. After a deadlock over procedure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-joseph-sieyes">Abbé Sieyès</a> moved on 28 May that the Third Estate proceed alone, and on 17 June it declared itself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. It took the Tennis Court Oath on 20 June, and on 9 July it reconstituted itself as the National Constituent Assembly.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estates General (France)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Estates-General</b>, the kingdom's assembly of clergy, nobles and commons, did not meet at all between 1614 and 1789, when its summons opened the Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
